  ('3c6d981a-8724-5fa8-9992-ddb305d59514', '1e6d4676-7fae-588c-b3f4-0c49cc40b721', 'https://www.pecva.org/region/loudoun/att-tower-proposal-threatens-scenic-ridgeline-and-violates-county-code/', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P4 | SEARCH-SNIPPET-ONLY] I SAW: titles and URLs, plus summary text on the comprehensive-plan conflict and the community campaign. I INFER: that these organisations worked from the public county file. Advocacy organisations opposing the application are interested parties; under §3.3 their material is not solicited-by-claimant with respect to this register, but it is partisan and should be read as a pointer to the county file rather than as an independent account.'),
  ('a760753a-10d9-5f34-b91a-25a503c5cf2d', '53cf876c-6ec0-591c-b6b2-cfb58fe549eb', 'https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/att-suspends-plans-for-secret-construction-on-short-hill-mountain-va/', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P4 | SEARCH-SNIPPET-ONLY] I SAW: titles, URLs, and summary text including the framing that the published specifications ''caused many to believe that the project was a data center in disguise - despite repeated claims to the contrary'', plus the 2 million gallons annual water figure. I INFER: standard trade-press practice of working from the public file and from vendor material. Valuable specifically because industry trade press reads these specifications as ordinary data-centre parameters, which is an informed reading of the mundane alternative from people who see such filings routinely.'),
  ('679fe476-38b0-5ea5-8abe-9bcd71d0f0d3', 'f591cdd3-e138-5762-8831-ab4a1268636e', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Offices', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P4 | FETCH-BLOCKED] I ATTEMPTED a direct fetch of the ''Project Offices'' article and received EGRESS_BLOCKED. I SAW only search-tool summaries, which carried the AT&T ''central facilities'' non-disclosure, the Chatham ''department-store sized / copper-covered / 30 feet / shock-absorbing springs / rubber plumbing'' resident descriptions, the Chatham 1966 in-service date, and the Peters Mountain $61 million permit figure. I could NOT see the article''s reference list, which is what I most wanted, because the reference list is where the $61M attribution and the resident-testimony attribution would be pinned. Crowd-edited, hence P4 and CROWD-EDITED under §2.4.'),
  ('d5a0175f-e7d2-5708-b9b6-7038ecc5101e', '58a575fb-6a60-59b5-95aa-14a87067f20f', 'https://indyweek.com/archives/archives-news/big-hole-deep-secret/', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P3 | SEARCH-SNIPPET-ONLY] I SAW: the title, author, publication, date and URL, plus a search-tool summary carrying specific figures ($27,000 land purchase, four-year $7 million construction, Durham''s Muirhead Construction Company, multi-floor 100,000 sq ft, 45 years of operation, AUTOVON). I INFER: that these figures come from county records and named local interviews, on the basis of their character; the article''s actual sourcing I have not seen. This is the most important single citation on the beat and the one most worth resolving first. Assigned P3 as bylined investigative journalism in a named alternative weekly.'),
  ('25c5d0e0-f984-567d-84a7-e3dcf67bac6a', 'f92f1e6f-7469-59e4-81ec-30ed46915107', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/23337486.2020.1850119', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P3 | SEARCH-SNIPPET-ONLY] I SAW: title, author, journal, volume/issue, DOI, and a one-sentence abstract summary describing Big Hole as ''a covert facility operated by AT&T in Chatham County, North Carolina, which was one of a small network of continuity of government sites''. I INFER: nothing about its evidence base, which from the title and journal is likely memoir-inflected and cultural rather than documentary. Peer-reviewed venue, hence P3. Paywalled; a resolved DOI is not a resolved article.'),
  ('066d2468-4aad-50bc-9e6f-10ce7c3c0663', 'bebea73a-9aa2-5ba9-885e-bfa1ec43342c', 'https://clui.org/ludb/site/att-chatham-big-hole-site', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P4 | SEARCH-SNIPPET-ONLY] I SAW: entry titles and search-tool summaries quoting descriptive passages. I INFER: that CLUI conducted site visits and photography. CLUI entries are unsigned and carry no citations in anything I saw, which is why I have assigned P4 (PENDING, treated as T4 per §3.2) rather than P3 despite the organisation''s standing. Critically, CLUI''s framing language on the Project Offices is close enough to LaFrance''s that under the §5.1.7 default it should collapse into his lineage until someone establishes its sourcing.'),
  ('4ae269c3-15a2-5c8e-8094-424bcfa17fad', '75ff0246-a304-54f1-92a3-abe641e5d3f6', 'https://coldwar-c4i.net/', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P4 | SEARCH-SNIPPET-ONLY] I SAW: page titles, URLs, and search-tool prose summarising the site''s contents. I INFER: that the site contains per-site pages with the described feature sets (earth-covered buildings, drive-through decontamination bays, tropo reflectors, helipads), a stated five-station chain, and hedged inferential language about a continuity-of-government mission. This is the origin of most of the beat''s substantive claims and I have not read one word of it directly. Author identified as Albert LaFrance via a third-party business-directory result, itself unverified. Assigned P4 per the source registry''s explicit ruling on this host; the registry directs that its claims be sourced backwards rather than credited.'),
  ('f712efef-f4db-5765-8951-08e1a63c0439', 'a19063ed-9098-58de-b76a-c97dd0aa8ea0', 'https://www.markturner.net/2008/09/24/secret-chatham-att-bunker-closes/', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P4 | SEARCH-SNIPPET-ONLY] I SAW: title, date, URL. I INFER: a contemporaneous personal blog report of the 2008 shutdown. Valuable chiefly as a DATED artefact — a 2008 first-hand-ish observation is a status anchor even at P4, and dated observation is scarce on this beat.'),
  ('0b3ec975-e152-50af-a3ec-b3285e27f914', '43174f08-9ea7-57dc-af1f-86b5d3566a3d', 'http://www.coldwarcomms.org/greenbrier/greenbrier0002.html', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P4 | SEARCH-SNIPPET-ONLY] I SAW: a URL and a search-tool statement that the Greenbrier switchboard bears those two legends. I INFER: that a photograph exists on that page showing them. I have NOT seen the photograph. THIS IS THE HIGHEST-DIAGNOSTICITY ITEM ON THE BEAT and it currently rests on a sentence in a search summary. It is also the most cheaply verifiable — the Greenbrier bunker is publicly toured, the switchboard is on display, and any resolvable named visitor with a camera can receipt it. Flagged as the priority acquisition for the Chatham and Hagerstown FUNCTION propositions.'),
  ('94ff5864-7fb9-5c64-a205-441b3bd4ed81', '7e8009e6-514f-50ef-8367-59d90f2e508f', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Offices', 'UNRESOLVED', 'code', '[P4 | FETCH-BLOCKED] I ATTEMPTED a direct fetch of the ''Project Offices'' article and received EGRESS_BLOCKED. I SAW only search-tool summaries, which carried the AT&T ''central facilities'' non-disclosure, the Chatham ''department-store sized / copper-covered / 30 feet / shock-absorbing springs / rubber plumbing'' resident descriptions, the Chatham 1966 in-service date, and the Peters Mountain $61 million permit figure. I could NOT see the article''s reference list, which is what I most wanted, because the reference list is where the $61M attribution and the resident-testimony attribution would be pinned. Crowd-edited, hence P4 and CROWD-EDITED under §2.4.'),
