values
  ('35203ed2-89d2-5e76-b110-1abb56d99dc5', 'A08', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A08 — an ordinary earth-bermed or partially below-grade federal office building of late-1960s design on a large federal campus, whose Cold War name reflects the agency that happened to occupy it rather than any protective engineering. This null is genuinely strong: the Denver Federal Center is a sprawling multi-building campus assembled from a wartime ordnance plant, and it contains a great deal of unremarkable government construction. What would distinguish it: the NRHP nomination''s statement of significance, or the USACE design record, specifying a design overpressure, blast closures, shielding or CBR filtration. What would NOT distinguish it: earth cover, reinforced concrete, absence of windows, a generator, or the building''s Cold War-era name. | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
