  ('6fc6a029-d2d3-5626-a7ab-07c1953c46f6', 'A10', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A10 — a federal office building whose below-grade levels carry a civil-defence fallout-shelter designation and nothing more, with ''bunker'' being a local honorific applied to an ordinary basement. This null is weakened, though not excluded, by the 13-ton door: fallout-shelter designation required shielding mass, not blast closures, and licensed shelter space in ordinary basements was enormously common. What would distinguish it: the door''s engineering rating, or an OCD construction contract as opposed to an OCD shelter-survey licence. What would NOT distinguish it: being underground, thick walls, ventilation equipment, a generator, or a shelter sign. | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
  ('b367b925-129f-5e47-9d5f-219e9751c86b', 'A12', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A12 — a decommissioned Nike missile installation whose existing magazines and control structures were refurbished into ordinary underground office space, with the ''bunker'' character inherited from the Nike works rather than purpose-built. This is the strongest surviving alternative and it has real teeth, because the site genuinely was a Nike installation and Nike magazines are genuinely subsurface reinforced concrete. One source explicitly describes the facility as ''an underground office facility located in a refurbished decommissioned Nike missile site'', which if accurate substantially undercuts the purpose-built reading. What would distinguish it: a 1968 new-construction contract or drawing set, as against a refurbishment contract. The reported $1.86M 1968 cost and the 120-by-140 footprint are the levers — a Nike launcher magazine has a known standard geometry and 120 by 140 feet does not match it, which is a concrete discriminating test. What would NOT distinguish it: being underground, being on a former military site, having blast-type doors (Nike magazines had them). | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
  ('0c0f13d6-3f83-5744-a3f9-64ab1d038f32', 'A12', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A12 — a decommissioned Nike missile installation whose existing magazines and control structures were refurbished into ordinary underground office space, with the ''bunker'' character inherited from the Nike works rather than purpose-built. This is the strongest surviving alternative and it has real teeth, because the site genuinely was a Nike installation and Nike magazines are genuinely subsurface reinforced concrete. One source explicitly describes the facility as ''an underground office facility located in a refurbished decommissioned Nike missile site'', which if accurate substantially undercuts the purpose-built reading. What would distinguish it: a 1968 new-construction contract or drawing set, as against a refurbishment contract. The reported $1.86M 1968 cost and the 120-by-140 footprint are the levers — a Nike launcher magazine has a known standard geometry and 120 by 140 feet does not match it, which is a concrete discriminating test. What would NOT distinguish it: being underground, being on a former military site, having blast-type doors (Nike magazines had them). | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
  ('05c60bb2-9547-5200-9359-90900a2120d3', 'A12', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A12 — a decommissioned Nike missile installation whose existing magazines and control structures were refurbished into ordinary underground office space, with the ''bunker'' character inherited from the Nike works rather than purpose-built. This is the strongest surviving alternative and it has real teeth, because the site genuinely was a Nike installation and Nike magazines are genuinely subsurface reinforced concrete. One source explicitly describes the facility as ''an underground office facility located in a refurbished decommissioned Nike missile site'', which if accurate substantially undercuts the purpose-built reading. What would distinguish it: a 1968 new-construction contract or drawing set, as against a refurbishment contract. The reported $1.86M 1968 cost and the 120-by-140 footprint are the levers — a Nike launcher magazine has a known standard geometry and 120 by 140 feet does not match it, which is a concrete discriminating test. What would NOT distinguish it: being underground, being on a former military site, having blast-type doors (Nike magazines had them). | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
  ('e8e334bd-7bc1-5a95-af09-929686ca04cd', 'A08', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A08 — an ordinary partially below-grade federal office building whose ''bunker'' description is retrospective, with fallout-shelter-grade rather than blast-grade protection. Massachusetts has a dense population of genuine Cold War hardened sites (the Hadley PACCS facility, Nike sites, Air Force stations), and the regional habit of calling any government structure with earth on it a bunker is strong. What would distinguish it: a construction record with a protective specification, or the 1969 handover documentation showing what was actually built. What would NOT distinguish it: roof antennas (every regional emergency office has them), being underground, or proximity to Boston. | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
