  ('15b2d07a-9953-5963-a062-6c312b1d4ce4', 'A08', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A08/A06 — a genuine USDA agricultural research station and former cavalry remount depot, on which the State Department was an ordinary co-located federal tenant occupying some surface buildings for contingency office space, with no concealment and no hardening. This null accounts for every reported physical fact: the buildings, the stockpile, the communications station, even the highway improvements (Warren County sits on the Skyline Drive corridor and had independent reasons for road investment). What would distinguish the concealment reading: a State Department, GSA or interagency record describing the arrangement, or an emergency-relocation designation document naming the site. What would NOT distinguish it: federal ownership, remoteness, distance from Washington, the presence of a radio station, or the site''s later Smithsonian use. | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
  ('6ab2b49e-1233-5632-bf74-2baa6431943c', 'A05', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A05/A07 — an ordinary mountaintop microwave relay and public-safety radio site, of which the eastern US ridgelines carry hundreds, whose Cold War federal ownership and hilltop compound are entirely unremarkable, with the ''presidential bunker'' reading supplied retrospectively by veterans'' recollection and enthusiast documentation. This null explains the tower, the compound, the access road, the fencing, the federal ownership and the current agency use without any bunker at all. What would distinguish it: an Army Signal Agency or WHCA construction or engineering record; a surveyable subsurface structure; or documented living quarters. What would NOT distinguish it: the tower, the microwave dishes, the summit location, the compound, the road, or the fact that the government owns it. A fire-tower conversion on a state-forest summit is the single most ordinary structure in Appalachian Maryland. | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
