  ('6027b599-c9c9-5b1b-ad12-a02cdcf0318c', 'A11', true, 'selected-strongest', 'A11, a fabricated or performed claim, with a specific and named physical substrate: an existing abandoned gold-mine shaft northwest of Ellensburg on private land, perhaps 300 feet deep, identified by the state DNR geologist as something the caller may have known about. What would distinguish: a Kittitas County parcel record for any owner named Waters in the relevant period, a Washington DNR or MSHA mine record for a shaft at the claimed location, or a lidar return over Manastash Ridge showing an anomalous aperture. Washington has substantial lidar coverage and this is a genuinely runnable test. | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
