  ('c4987f1f-4ba8-5ec1-b19c-9dde905cf385', 'A11', true, 'selected-strongest', 'For EXIST there is a real and interesting null here, which is rare: A01 — ''no substantial enclosed structure remains; the launch duct was collapsed and backfilled and what survives is a filled void and surface pads, not a structure.'' This matters because it forces EXIST and STAT apart in a way the register should want demonstrated. What would distinguish them: the Air Force disposal and site-closure documentation describing what was actually done to the duct; the NRHP nomination, which had to describe the extant resource to justify listing; a geophysical survey. Aerial imagery showing pads does not distinguish them. | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
