  ('4c1dbebb-1308-5224-91d3-a294efb69d13', 'A11', true, 'selected-strongest', 'For EXIST/TYPO: A12 — ''a decommissioned facility of a different, already-known typology'', specifically an ordinary former Army administrative post whose ''magazines'' are surface storage buildings rather than subsurface missile magazines. This sounds pedantic and is not: the distinguishing feature of a Nike LAUNCH site is the underground magazine with its missile elevator, and many former Nike parcels retain only the control-site buildings while the launch area was filled or sold separately. What would distinguish it: the Army site list''s launch/control site split for BA-79; the state property record''s description; HAER documentation, since many Nike sites were HAER-recorded with measured drawings deposited at the Library of Congress; or a photograph of an elevator in a magazine. Reports of tours ''inside cavernous magazines with metal elevators'' are direct-observation support and are the strongest thing I have. | null_state as graded: SURVIVING. DERIVED per 4.6, and VACUOUS. A named null is present so the state is not UNTESTED; DOMINANT requires the null to be affirmatively documented by a verified T1/T2 row and none exists; INSUFFICIENT and EXCLUDED require verified rows in V at D2+/D3+ and V is empty. SURVIVING therefore holds only because the null trivially accounts for all zero rows in V. This must not be read as a finding that the mundane explanation is strong.', 'claude-opus-5 (W1 grading pass)'),
